      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "As someone who gets creeped out when someone starts listing anything about my systems performance or specs. DON'T DO THIS. This is one thing that I am glad is gone. Last thing I want is for someone to be able to tell my framerate/ping or anything else about my system before I can even take a glance at it myself. If I could, I would downvote this...",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-07-31T23:02:40.534Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Hello,\nI feel like having the ability to show remote player's ping, framerate, and photon master status above their nameplate (likely as a toggle) could be beneficial, and I know is also a feature some mods used to have.\nHaving this information is beneficial in my opinion for different reasons:\nPING:\nFor ping, seeing a rapid increase in remote player ping while you are talking to them can indicate they are suffering a network issue, and can preemptively prompt you to pause you speaking with them until the network conditions are better, or could encourage you to repeat yourself so they didn't miss anything. Additionally, seeing everyone else around you have a high ping may mean it could be a good idea to create a new instance in a different region. Often sometimes players don't realize what region they're connected to and seeing high pings all around could be useful for this.\nFRAMERATE:\nSeeing remote framerates also has similar benefits to remote ping. If a player encounters a sudden framedrop due to connecting, or just new avatars loading in, you can pause interacting with them until their performance stabilizes. I know when first joining an instance I can't interact with everyone until all avatars load and stop causing framedrops, so having others able to tell when I'm \"ready\" is beneficial.\nPHOTON MASTER:\nThis one is definitely more niche but I feel is still important. Some worlds still heavily depend on who the photon master is in order for certain things to function (video player locking, etc) but don't always explicitly state who it is. Currently, I don't think there's any official way to determine who the photon master is. Having an indicator in their nameplate may help this. (Also, Indicating instance owner, who may not also be the photon master, could be a good addition as well)",
